WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.293 --> 00:00:07.234
When Monsieur Gillenormand found the cards and locket belonging to Marius, he was furious.

2
00:00:07.235 --> 00:00:11.118
He sat in silence for an hour until Marius returned home.

3
00:00:11.119 --> 00:00:16.539
Then, as soon as his grandson opened the door, he lashed out at the young man.

4
00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:25.340
"So you’re a baron now?" Monsieur Gillenormand held up one of Marius’ calling cards. "What does this mean?"

5
00:00:25.341 --> 00:00:29.697
"It means that I am my father’s son," replied Marius.

6
00:00:29.698 --> 00:00:37.269
"Your father’s son?" Monsieur Gillenormand said harshly. "But I am your father."

7
00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:42.419
"My father was a humble and heroic man, who gloriously served France.

8
00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:50.419
He died forgotten and abandoned, and he had just one fault: He loved his country and me too much."

9
00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:55.795
This was more than Gillenormand could listen to. He sprang to his feet.

10
00:00:55.796 --> 00:00:58.832
"Marius, you abominable child!

11
00:00:58.833 --> 00:01:02.736
I don’t know what your father was, and I don’t want to know!

12
00:01:02.737 --> 00:01:10.649
What I do know is that the men fighting for Napoleon were all beggars, assassins, and thieves! They were cowards and traitors!"

13
00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:13.705
Marius shuddered at his grandfather’s words.

14
00:01:13.706 --> 00:01:20.069
He had to defend his father, yet that would mean insulting his grandfather. How could he do that?

15
00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:24.236
"Down with King Louis XVIII!" he finally shouted.

16
00:01:24.237 --> 00:01:30.561
The king had been dead for four years, but it was the best insult that Marius could come up with at the moment.

17
00:01:30.562 --> 00:01:38.220
The old man turned whiter than his hair. "We cannot remain under the same roof. Be off!"

18
00:01:38.221 --> 00:01:44.212
Marius went away without saying where he was going, without knowing where he was going.

19
00:01:44.213 --> 00:01:53.196
He could find neither the locket nor his father’s "will," so he assumed that his grandfather had hidden them somewhere or thrown them into the fire.

20
00:01:53.197 --> 00:01:58.869
Marius left with thirty francs, his watch, and a few things in a carpetbag.

21
00:01:58.870 --> 00:02:03.934
He hired a cab, jumped in, and headed toward the Latin Quarter.

22
00:02:03.935 --> 00:02:09.767
When Marius arrived outside the Cafe Musain, he recognized a fellow law student.

23
00:02:09.768 --> 00:02:14.534
Although he did not know this student very well, Marius got out of the cab.

24
00:02:14.535 --> 00:02:19.442
His new friend, Courfeyrac, knew of a place where Marius could stay.

25
00:02:19.443 --> 00:02:22.926
It was in the lodging house where Courfeyrac lived.

26
00:02:22.927 --> 00:02:26.997
Courfeyrac also introduced Marius to a group of friends.

27
00:02:26.998 --> 00:02:34.013
They belonged to a secret society of students and workingmen who wanted to improve the lot of the common man.

28
00:02:34.014 --> 00:02:37.725
Marius started to attend their meetings at the Cafe Musain.

29
00:02:37.726 --> 00:02:43.076
He began to feel so comfortable with these young men that he joined in their debates.

30
00:02:43.077 --> 00:02:47.338
One afternoon he loudly defended Napoleon.

31
00:02:47.339 --> 00:02:50.648
His remarks were greeted with silence.

32
00:02:50.649 --> 00:02:56.481
It was obvious that he was a bigger supporter of the former emperor than the other students.

33
00:02:56.482 --> 00:03:00.794
They wanted to push for a republic with a leader elected by the people.

34
00:03:00.796 --> 00:03:06.148
So Marius now felt isolated from his new friends as well as his grandfather.

35
00:03:06.149 --> 00:03:13.061
But Courfeyrac remained his friend. When the landlord demanded the rent, Courfeyrac tried to help Marius.

36
00:03:13.062 --> 00:03:18.132
"What are you going to do?" asked Courfeyrac. "How much money do you have?"

37
00:03:18.133 --> 00:03:23.466
"I have no idea," said Marius. "I only have 15 francs."

38
00:03:23.467 --> 00:03:26.115
"I could lend you the rent money."

39
00:03:26.116 --> 00:03:29.212
"No, I don’t want to be in debt to anyone."

40
00:03:29.213 --> 00:03:36.450
Marius didn’t mention that his grandfather had asked his aunt to send him six hundred francs every six months.

41
00:03:36.451 --> 00:03:41.537
Marius had returned the money to her, saying that he had enough to live on.

42
00:03:41.538 --> 00:03:48.934
"I know a clothes dealer who’ll buy your overcoat and a pair of trousers," said Courfeyrac. "Do you have a watch?"

43
00:03:48.935 --> 00:03:58.434
"Yes, a gold one. If I sell the clothing, that will leave me with one pair of trousers, one waistcoat, a hat, and a coat. That’ll be enough."

44
00:03:58.435 --> 00:04:02.696
"I also know a watchmaker who’ll buy your watch," said Courfeyrac.

45
00:04:02.697 --> 00:04:07.150
"But what will you do after that? Can you read English or German?"

46
00:04:07.151 --> 00:04:08.513
"No."

47
00:04:08.514 --> 00:04:14.680
"That’s too bad because a friend of mine, a publisher, needs someone who can translate articles into French.

48
00:04:14.681 --> 00:04:18.505
It doesn’t pay well, but you can live on it."

49
00:04:18.506 --> 00:04:22.156
"Then I will learn English and German."

50
00:04:22.157 --> 00:04:26.497
Marius handed his overcoat, trousers, and watch to Courfeyrac.

51
00:04:26.498 --> 00:04:32.918
Life became hard for Marius. He moved to other lodgings, where he swept his own room.

52
00:04:32.919 --> 00:04:37.067
Sometimes he bought only a penny’s worth of cheese at the market.

53
00:04:37.068 --> 00:04:41.807
He waited until evening to buy bread when the baker reduced the price.

54
00:04:41.808 --> 00:04:47.402
When he bought a lamb cutlet, he cooked it himself and made it last three days.

55
00:04:47.403 --> 00:04:53.315
Through all this, Marius managed to study law and to be admitted to the bar.

56
00:04:53.316 --> 00:05:02.299
Three years passed. By means of hard work, courage, and perseverance, Marius managed to earn seven hundred francs a year.

57
00:05:02.300 --> 00:05:09.704
He had learned English and German, and thanks to Courfeyrac, he had a job at the publishing house as a translator.

58
00:05:09.705 --> 00:05:19.283
Marius had survived a world of deprivation. In his struggle against poverty, he had done everything except go into debt.

59
00:05:19.284 --> 00:05:21.942
He didn’t owe money to anyone.

60
00:05:21.943 --> 00:05:27.450
In all his trials he felt encouraged by a secret force within himself.

61
00:05:27.451 --> 00:05:34.224
Beside his father’s name, another name was engraved upon Marius’ heart: Thenardier.

62
00:05:34.225 --> 00:05:38.014
Marius had surrounded this man with a kind of halo.

63
00:05:38.015 --> 00:05:45.521
Thenardier was a hero, as far as Marius was concerned, because the innkeeper had saved his father’s life.

64
00:05:45.522 --> 00:05:49.256
Marius had even gone to Montfermeil in search of Thenardier.

65
00:05:49.257 --> 00:05:54.434
There he had learned of the unlucky innkeeper’s bankruptcy and ruin.

66
00:05:54.435 --> 00:05:59.338
Since then Marius had made many efforts to track the man down.

67
00:05:59.339 --> 00:06:05.989
For three years he had devoted himself to this search, spending what little money he could spare.

68
00:06:05.990 --> 00:06:11.997
But nobody could give him any news of Thenardier. It was thought that he had gone abroad.

69
00:06:11.998 --> 00:06:19.981
Even Thenardier’s creditors could not find him, and they had been looking for him with even more zeal than Marius.

70
00:06:19.982 --> 00:06:28.068
Marius’ father had left him only one debt to pay: to thank Thenardier and help the innkeeper in any way he could.

71
00:06:28.069 --> 00:06:32.131
And Marius had made it a point of honor to pay this debt.

72
00:06:32.132 --> 00:06:42.038
His only goal in life was to meet Thenardier one day and say, "You do not know me, but I know you and what you have done for my father.

73
00:06:42.039 --> 00:06:45.211
Here I am. Let me help you."

74
00:06:45.212 --> 00:06:50.116
As for Marius’ relationship with his grandfather, things remained the same.

75
00:06:50.117 --> 00:06:54.078
Neither side attempted a reconciliation or sought a meeting.

76
00:06:54.079 --> 00:06:59.711
Marius imagined that his grandfather had never loved him, but he was mistaken.

77
00:06:59.712 --> 00:07:09.370
Gillenormand adored Marius. He had ordered everyone to never speak of Marius again, but he regretted that he was so well obeyed.

78
00:07:09.371 --> 00:07:18.608
At first he was sure that Marius would return. But weeks, months, and years passed without Marius arriving home.

79
00:07:18.609 --> 00:07:24.672
Old men need affection as they do sunshine, and Gillenormand missed Marius.

80
00:07:24.673 --> 00:07:31.926
While Gillenormand was suffering, Marius was congratulating himself on never thinking about the old man.

81
00:07:31.927 --> 00:07:39.926
Suffering had taken away Marius’ bitterness. If he thought about his grandfather at all, it was only with kindness.

82
00:07:39.927 --> 00:07:46.594
But he had determined to take nothing more from the man who had been so cruel to his father.

83
00:07:46.595 --> 00:07:54.848
When his grandfather drove him away, Marius was a child. But poverty and suffering had made Marius a man.

